/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In DC the ratio V/I is R.
   - Each element gives one impedance.
   - Z is impedance in Ω; R is resistance in Ω; X is reactance in Ω; θ is the angle of Z in degrees, equal to the angle by which voltage leads current.
   - X is the part that stores and returns energy instead of burning it.
   - Series impedances add: Z = Z₁ + Z₂ + …, because the current is common.
   - Parallel admittances add: Y = Y₁ + Y₂ + …, or Z = Z₁Z₂/(Z₁ + Z₂) for two branches.
   - For example, 1/(6 + j8) = (6 − j8)/100 = 0.06 − j0.08 S.
   - Admittance is the reciprocal: Y = 1/Z = G + jB, in siemens (S); older texts say mho.
   - Frequency changes the picture. |ZL| = ωL grows with ω, so an inductor blocks high frequencies. |ZC| = 1/(ωC) shrinks, so a capacitor passes them.
*/
