/* book Worked example 2: Q: The same coil (8 Ω, 0.02 H) and the same 100 µF capacitor are now connected in parallel across the 230 V, 50 Hz supply. Find the admittance, the equivalent impedance and the supply current.
   1. Coil branch: Z1 = 8 + j6.28 Ω, |Z1|² = 64 + 39.48 = 103.48 Ω².
   2. Y1 = 1/Z1 = (8 − j6.28)/103.48 = 0.0773 − j0.0607 S.
   3. Capacitor branch: Y2 = jωC = j314.16 × 100 × 10⁻⁶ = j0.0314 S.
   4. Parallel admittances add: Y = Y1 + Y2 = 0.0773 − j0.0293 S. |Y| = √(0.0773² + 0.0293²) = 0.0827 S at −20.8°.
   5. Z = 1/Y = 12.10 Ω at +20.8°, that is 11.31 + j4.29 Ω: the parallel pair is now slightly inductive.
   6. I = V × |Y| = 230 × 0.0827 = 19.0 A, lagging the supply by 20.8°. The branch currents are 230/10.17 = 22.6 A in the coil and 230 × 0.0314 = 7.2 A in the capacitor.
   Answer: Y = 0.0773 − j0.0293 S, Z = 12.10∠20.8° Ω, I = 19.0 A lagging. The same three parts in series were capacitive and drew 8.59 A; in parallel the coil current (22.6 A) is larger than the supply current, because the capacitor current cancels part of it. */
