/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At a single frequency ω every voltage and current in a linear circuit is a sinusoid of that same frequency, so only magnitude and phase change from point to point.
   - Keep magnitudes as RMS.
   - Z is impedance in Ω; R is resistance in Ω; X is net reactance in Ω; ω is angular frequency in rad/s; L is inductance in H; C is capacitance in F; V and I are RMS phasors in V and A; θ is the impedance angle in degrees.
   - In a series circuit one current flows through all three elements, so you find I first and then multiply by each impedance.
   - The sign of θ tells you the story.
   - Two element voltages can each be bigger than the source.
   - In parallel, admittances add: Y = 1/R + jωC + 1/(jωL), and Z = 1/Y.
   - Laplace transform analysis solves the same circuit in full: a transient that decays like e−t/τ plus the steady sinusoid.
*/
