/* book Worked example: Q: A series RLC circuit has R = 40 Ω, L = 0.15 H and C = 50 µF. The source is v(t) = 230√2 sin(314.16t) V. Find i(t) and the RMS voltages across R, L and C, and check that the element voltages rebuild the source.
   1. ω = 314.16 rad/s. The source as an RMS phasor is V = 230∠0° V (peak 230√2 = 325.3 V).
   2. XL = ωL = 314.16 × 0.15 = 47.12 Ω.
   3. XC = 1/(ωC) = 1/(314.16 × 50×10⁻⁶) = 63.66 Ω.
   4. Net reactance X = XL − XC = 47.12 − 63.66 = −16.54 Ω. Negative, so the circuit is capacitive.
   5. Z = R + jX = 40 − j16.54 Ω. |Z| = √(40² + 16.54²) = 43.28 Ω, and θ = tan⁻¹(−16.54/40) = −22.5°.
   6. I = V/Z = 230∠0° ÷ 43.28∠−22.5° = 5.31∠+22.5° A RMS. Peak = 5.31 × 1.414 = 7.51 A.
   7. The current angle is 0 − (−22.5°) = +22.5°, so the current leads the source: i(t) = 7.51 sin(314.16t + 22.5°) A.
   8. VR = I R = 5.31 × 40 = 212.5 V, in phase with I.
   9. VL = I XL = 5.31 × 47.12 = 250.4 V, leading I by 90°.
   10. VC = I XC = 5.31 × 63.66 = 338.3 V, lagging I by 90°.
   11. Magnitude check: √(212.5² + (250.4 − 338.3)²) = √(45156 + 7726) = √52882 = 230.0 V, the source. VL and VC individually exceed 230 V but nearly cancel.
   Answer: i(t) = 7.51 sin(314.16t + 22.5°) A, with VR = 212.5 V, VL = 250.4 V and VC = 338.3 V RMS. */
