/* book Worked example 2: Q: A coil of resistance 6 Ω and reactance 8 Ω is connected in parallel with a capacitor of reactance 20 Ω across a 230 V, 50 Hz supply. Find each branch current, the supply current, the power factor and the power drawn.
   1. Take the supply as the reference: V = 230∠0° V RMS. The voltage is common to both branches, so find each branch current from V.
   2. Coil: Z1 = 6 + j8 = 10∠53.13° Ω. I1 = 230∠0°/10∠53.13° = 23.0∠−53.13° A = 13.8 − j18.4 A.
   3. Capacitor: Z2 = −j20 = 20∠−90° Ω. I2 = 230∠0°/20∠−90° = 11.5∠90° A = j11.5 A.
   4. KCL with phasors: I = I1 + I2 = 13.8 − j18.4 + j11.5 = 13.8 − j6.9 A.
   5. |I| = √(13.8² + 6.9²) = √(190.44 + 47.61) = 15.43 A, at tan⁻¹(−6.9/13.8) = −26.57°: the current lags.
   6. Power factor = cos 26.57° = 0.894 lagging. Equivalent impedance Z = 230/15.43 = 14.91 Ω at 26.57°.
   7. Power: P = V I cos φ = 230 × 15.43 × 0.894 = 3174 W. Check: only the 6 Ω resistance takes power, I1² R = 23.0² × 6 = 3174 W.
   Answer: I1 = 23.0∠−53.13° A, I2 = 11.5∠90° A, I = 15.43∠−26.57° A, power factor 0.894 lagging, P = 3174 W. The supply current is smaller than the coil current because the capacitor current cancels part of its lagging component. */
