/* book exam traps: Stopping at Z or I in complex form. The examiner wants the time function with its phase; a bare 5.31∠22.5° A can lose half the marks. | Subtracting VL from VC as if the two were in phase. They are 180° apart, so they oppose as phasors; written as 250.4 − 338.3 they only look like a subtraction because both are taken along the same axis. | Mixing peak and RMS. Phasors are kept in RMS so they match meter readings, and √2 is applied only when writing i(t). Mixing the two scales gives answers wrong by 41%. | Treating a real coil as pure jωL. Its winding resistance must sit in the same impedance, so the coil angle is below 90°. | Reversing lead and lag. Check the sign: X > 0 means inductive and the current lags; X < 0 means capacitive and the current leads. */
